  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Dry weather will prevail today through most of \ntonight with warmer temperatures than the past few days.\n\nPatchy fog across the Southern Tier, Finger Lakes, and east of Lake \nOntario will continue through mid morning before dissipating. \nOtherwise, surface high pressure over NY/PA this morning will drift \noff the New England coast this afternoon, supporting dry weather \ntoday. Warm advection and height rises will allow for warmer \ntemperatures than the past few days, with highs today in the upper \n70s to lower 80s for lower elevations and mid 70s for higher \nterrain. \n\nDry weather will continue to prevail through most of tonight. A mid \nlevel trough and associated cold front will move through the central \nGreat Lakes. Clouds will thicken and lower overnight ahead of the \napproaching system. The first of the scattered showers and \nthunderstorms may arrive in far Western NY just before daybreak \nThursday.\n\nKey Message 2...A cold front will bring a few showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday.\n\nA broad mid level trough will advance east across Ontario and Quebec \nThursday, with height falls spreading east across the Great Lakes \nand into New England. A surface pre-frontal trough will arrive in \nWestern NY Thursday morning, then reach Central NY and the eastern \nLake Ontario region by early to mid afternoon. The actual cold front \nand more notable dewpoint boundary will cross the eastern Great \nLakes Thursday evening. The majority of the active weather will be \nfound along the pre-frontal trough rather than the cold front, as is \noften the case here in the warm season.\n\nThe early arrival of the pre-frontal trough and associated \nclouds/showers will greatly limit potential destabilization, \nespecially across Western NY. The eastern edge of the CWA across \nCentral NY and the eastern Lake Ontario region may have enough \ndaylight ahead of the trough for modest destabilization by midday as \nsurface dewpoints briefly climb into the mid to upper 60s and \ntemperatures reach the lower 80s. \n\nLarge scale height falls and DPVA ahead of the trough combined with \nlow level convergence along the advancing pre-frontal trough will \nsupport a broken line of scattered to numerous showers and a few \nthunderstorms moving from west to east across the region Thursday. \nThe majority of the rain will be in the first half of the day across \nWestern NY, and midday into the afternoon across Central NY and east \nof Lake Ontario. A few additional widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms may develop along lake breeze boundaries Thursday \nafternoon across Western NY, but an expanding stable lake shadow \nwill keep the Buffalo Metro area mainly dry in the afternoon. \n\nAs far as severe weather potential goes, the early arrival of the \npre-frontal trough and resulting lack of destabilization will \ngreatly limit the potential across Western NY. Farther east, modest \ndestabilization and modest deep layer shear may support some low end \nsevere potential, with the main risk being isolated damaging wind \ngusts across Central NY and the eastern Lake Ontario region.\n\nKey Message 3...Dry weather will return Friday and last through the \nweekend.\n\nA broad mid level trough will advance east across Quebec Friday. A \ncooler airmass rounding into the base of the trough may support a \nfew scattered lake effect rain showers east of Lake Ontario in the \nmorning, but these will end by afternoon as surface high pressure \nbuilds into the eastern Great Lakes with lowering inversion heights \nand a drying airmass.\n\nThe surface high will linger across the eastern Great Lakes and New \nEngland Saturday, then drift off the east coast Sunday. This surface \nhigh and building heights aloft will support mainly dry weather \nthrough the weekend. An initially cool airmass Friday and Friday \nnight will give way to a day to day warming trend, with above \naverage temperatures returning Sunday into early next week.",
